---
const { data } = Astro.props;
const repo = 'https://github.com/wbniv/llvm-mos-65816/blob/main/';
---
<section class="svx-tech" aria-labelledby="svx-tech-title">
  <h2 id="svx-tech-title">The cartridge, measured</h2>
  <p class="svx-kicker">This is a complete player result, not a desktop encoder estimate.</p>
  <div class="svx-stats">
    {data.stats.map((s) => <div><strong>{s.value}</strong><span>{s.label}</span></div>)}
  </div>

  <h2>How one frame moves</h2>
  <figure class="svx-pipeline">
    <div>FastROM packet table<small>code mirror + stream in 64 Mbit (8 MiB) ExHiROM</small></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
    <div>Bank $7F staging<small>DMA during active display</small></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
    <div>SVX2 in-place decode<small>replacement + retained spans</small></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
    <div>Mode 7 VRAM<small>4,480-byte DMA in VBlank</small></div>
    <figcaption>Packet staging and decode happen during active display; VBlank is reserved for presentation and the small BG3 dashboard update.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>Each frame is 70 tile-major Mode 7 tiles: 80 × 56 indexed pixels. A replacement span copies changed bytes from the staged packet. A copy span means “retain what is already in the framebuffer,” so the hot decoder advances its cursors without copying bytes onto themselves. Sequential playback stays entirely delta-coded—including each cut and the loop—while separate independent keyframes keep seeking bounded. Frame 1200 begins exactly at the ExHiROM region seam: logical <code>$3F0000</code> becomes file <code>$410000</code> / CPU bank <code>$41</code>.</p>
  <p>Mode 7 owns scanlines 0–191. An HDMA split switches the lower region to Mode 1 BG3 for the time, measured FPS, scene label, health, and transport. Automatic joypad results are read from <code>$4218/$4219</code> off the decode-critical path.</p>

  <h2>Why SVX2?</h2>
  <div class="svx-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable codec benchmark table">
    <table><caption>Codec-selection benchmark corpus. Size includes packet overhead; the published native-60 reel is independently gated below.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Codec or path</th><th>Packed size</th><th>Raw ratio</th><th>65816 rate</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{data.codecs.map((c) => <tr class:list={{ selected: c.selected }}><th scope="row">{c.name}</th><td>{c.size}</td><td>{c.ratio}</td><td>{c.rate}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>SVX1 wins on size, but its target decoder misses even the 20 fps goal. SVX2 is the smallest tested representation whose target-native decoder clears the cadence requirement. Gallery LZSS is shown only as an independent-frame baseline: at roughly two frames per second, it is not the video codec.</p>

  <h2>Full-pipeline timing</h2>
  <div class="svx-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable pipeline timing table">
    <table><caption>After the in-place-copy optimization; one NTSC interval is {data.intervalDots.toLocaleString()} PPU dots.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>p50</th><th>p99</th><th>Maximum</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{data.phases.map((p) => <tr class:list={{ selected: p.name === 'Combined' }}><th scope="row">{p.name}</th><td>{p.p50.toLocaleString()}</td><td>{p.p99.toLocaleString()}</td><td>{p.maximum.toLocaleString()}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <figure class="svx-budget"><div><span style={`width:${(89088 / data.intervalDots * 100).toFixed(2)}%`}></span></div><figcaption>p99 combined: 89,088 / {data.intervalDots.toLocaleString()} dots. The 275,456-dot maximum is a seek keyframe, intentionally absent from sequential playback.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>The release gate presented <strong>9,000 frames in 9,000 eligible intervals</strong> after the animated title, with zero decoder errors and zero deadline slips. The source is NASA SVS’s 60/1-fps XRISM master; all <strong>1,800 of 1,800</strong> quantized SNES frames are unique, with no 30-fps doubling or synthesized intermediate frames.</p>
  <p>The cartridge dashboard reports the nominal playback cadence as <strong>60.0 fps</strong>. Physical NTSC SNES refresh is approximately 60.099 Hz; that hardware-rate detail belongs in timing analysis, not in the player’s nominal-cadence readout.</p>

  <h2>Verification and source</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Host round-trip checks every sequential packet and seek keyframe.</li>
    <li>Target gates compare all 4,480 decoded bytes and monitor decode, loop, and deadline state.</li>
    <li>Rendezvous captures cover frames 1199 and 1200 on opposite sides of the ExHiROM seam, the dashboard, and exact frame order.</li>
    <li>Controller replay covers pause, frame step, ±1-second seek, shuttle, resume, and both directions across the seam.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="svx-links"><a href={`${repo}examples/snes/snes-video-reel.c`}>Player scheduler</a><a href={`${repo}examples/snes/snes-video-reel-fast.s`}>65816 hot path</a><a href={`${repo}tools/snes_video_codec.py`}>Codec implementation</a><a href={`${repo}docs/investigations/2026-08-01-svx2-60-fps-pipeline-profile.md`}>Full profile</a><a href={`${repo}docs/plans/2026-07-30-lzss-gallery-exhirom-video-boundary-test/real-video-codec-benchmark.md`}>Codec corpus</a></p>
  <p class="svx-artifact">{data.artifactLabel}<br><code>{data.artifactSha256}</code></p>
</section>
